  "client_view_text": " This will help you understand the process and make adjustments as needed to achieve your perfect cup.\nStep 1: Prepare the Pour-Over Equipment\nGather your pour-over equipment, including the dripper, filter, and coffee grounds. Rinse the filter with hot water to remove any paper taste and preheat the dripper.\nStep 2: Measure and Grind the Coffee\nMeasure out the right amount of coffee for your pour-over, typically around 1:15 to 1:17 coffee-to-water ratio. Grind the coffee beans to a medium-coarse grind, suitable for pour-over brewing.\nStep 3: Heat This will help you understand the process and make adjustments as needed to achieve your perfect cup.\nStep 1: Prepare the Pour-Over Equipment\nGather your pour-over equipment, including the dripper, filter, and coffee grounds. Rinse the filter with hot water to remove any paper taste and preheat the dripper.\nStep 2: Measure and Grind the Coffee\nMeasure out the right amount of coffee for your pour-over, typically around 1:15 to 1:17 coffee-to-water ratio. Grind the coffee beans to a medium-coarse grind, suitable for pour-over brewing.\nStep 3: Heat the Water\nHeat water to the ideal temperature for brewing coffee, between 195\u00b0F and 205\u00b0F. Use a thermometer to ensure the water reaches the correct temperature.\nStep 4: Bloom the Coffee\nPlace the ground coffee in the filter and pour a small amount of hot water over it to \"bloom\" the coffee. This allows the coffee to release its CO2 and start the extraction process. Wait for about 45 seconds to 1 minute.\nStep 5: Pour the Rest of the Water\nSlowly pour the rest of the hot water over the coffee in a circular motion, making sure to evenly saturate all"
